            {
              "id": "GHSA-965h-392x-2mh5",
              "name": "LanguageSpecificPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "webpki: Name constraints for URI names were incorrectly accepted"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Name constraints for URI names were ignored and therefore accepted.\n\nNote this library does not provide an API for asserting URI names, and URI name constraints are otherwise not implemented.  URI name constraints are now rejected unconditionally.\n\nSince name constraints are restrictions on otherwise properly-issued certificates, this bug is reachable only after signature verification and requires misissuance to exploit."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-965h-392x-2mh5",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability GHSA-965h-392x-2mh5\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: rustls-webpki\nFixed Version: 0.103.12, 0.104.0-alpha.6\nLink: [GHSA-965h-392x-2mh5](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-965h-392x-2mh5)\nName constraints for URI names were ignored and therefore accepted.\n\nNote this library does not provide an API for asserting URI names, and URI name constraints are otherwise not implemented.  URI name constraints are now rejected unconditionally.\n\nSince name constraints are restrictions on otherwise properly-issued certificates, this bug is reachable only after signature verification and requires misissuance to exploit.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability GHSA-965h-392x-2mh5**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|rustls-webpki|0.103.12, 0.104.0-alpha.6|[GHSA-965h-392x-2mh5](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-965h-392x-2mh5)|\n\nName constraints for URI names were ignored and therefore accepted.\n\nNote this library does not provide an API for asserting URI names, and URI name constraints are otherwise not implemented.  URI name constraints are now rejected unconditionally.\n\nSince name constraints are restrictions on otherwise properly-issued certificates, this bug is reachable only after signature verification and requires misissuance to exploit."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cvssv3_baseScore": 2.2,
                "cvssv3_vector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:H/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:L/A:N",
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "2.2",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "GHSA-xgp8-3hg3-c2mh",
              "name": "LanguageSpecificPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "webpki: Name constraints were accepted for certificates asserting a wildcard name"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Permitted subtree name constraints for DNS names were accepted for certificates asserting a wildcard name.\n\nThis was incorrect because, given a name constraint of `accept.example.com`, `*.example.com` could feasibly allow a name of `reject.example.com` which is outside the constraint.\nThis is very similar to [CVE-2025-61727](https://go.dev/issue/76442).\n\nSince name constraints are restrictions on otherwise properly-issued certificates, this bug is reachable only after signature verification and requires misissuance to exploit."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-xgp8-3hg3-c2mh",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability GHSA-xgp8-3hg3-c2mh\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: rustls-webpki\nFixed Version: 0.103.12, 0.104.0-alpha.6\nLink: [GHSA-xgp8-3hg3-c2mh](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-xgp8-3hg3-c2mh)\nPermitted subtree name constraints for DNS names were accepted for certificates asserting a wildcard name.\n\nThis was incorrect because, given a name constraint of `accept.example.com`, `*.example.com` could feasibly allow a name of `reject.example.com` which is outside the constraint.\nThis is very similar to [CVE-2025-61727](https://go.dev/issue/76442).\n\nSince name constraints are restrictions on otherwise properly-issued certificates, this bug is reachable only after signature verification and requires misissuance to exploit.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability GHSA-xgp8-3hg3-c2mh**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|rustls-webpki|0.103.12, 0.104.0-alpha.6|[GHSA-xgp8-3hg3-c2mh](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-xgp8-3hg3-c2mh)|\n\nPermitted subtree name constraints for DNS names were accepted for certificates asserting a wildcard name.\n\nThis was incorrect because, given a name constraint of `accept.example.com`, `*.example.com` could feasibly allow a name of `reject.example.com` which is outside the constraint.\nThis is very similar to [CVE-2025-61727](https://go.dev/issue/76442).\n\nSince name constraints are restrictions on otherwise properly-issued certificates, this bug is reachable only after signature verification and requires misissuance to exploit."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cvssv3_baseScore": 2.2,
                "cvssv3_vector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:H/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:L/A:N",
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "2.2",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-25727",
              "name": "LanguageSpecificPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "time: time affected by a stack exhaustion denial of service attack"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "time provides date and time handling in Rust. From 0.3.6 to before 0.3.47, when user-provided input is provided to any type that parses with the RFC 2822 format, a denial of service attack via stack exhaustion is possible. The attack relies on formally deprecated and rarely-used features that are part of the RFC 2822 format used in a malicious manner. Ordinary, non-malicious input will never encounter this scenario. A limit to the depth of recursion was added in v0.3.47. From this version, an error will be returned rather than exhausting the stack."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-25727",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-25727\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: time\nFixed Version: 0.3.47\nLink: [CVE-2026-25727](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-25727)\ntime provides date and time handling in Rust. From 0.3.6 to before 0.3.47, when user-provided input is provided to any type that parses with the RFC 2822 format, a denial of service attack via stack exhaustion is possible. The attack relies on formally deprecated and rarely-used features that are part of the RFC 2822 format used in a malicious manner. Ordinary, non-malicious input will never encounter this scenario. A limit to the depth of recursion was added in v0.3.47. From this version, an error will be returned rather than exhausting the stack.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-25727**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|time|0.3.47|[CVE-2026-25727](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-25727)|\n\ntime provides date and time handling in Rust. From 0.3.6 to before 0.3.47, when user-provided input is provided to any type that parses with the RFC 2822 format, a denial of service attack via stack exhaustion is possible. The attack relies on formally deprecated and rarely-used features that are part of the RFC 2822 format used in a malicious manner. Ordinary, non-malicious input will never encounter this scenario. A limit to the depth of recursion was added in v0.3.47. From this version, an error will be returned rather than exhausting the stack."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cvssv40_baseScore": 6.8,
                "cvssv40_vector": "CVSS:4.0/AV:N/AC:H/AT:N/PR:L/UI:A/VC:N/VI:N/VA:H/SC:N/SI:N/SA:H",
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "MEDIUM"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "GHSA-rr8g-9fpq-6wmg",
              "name": "LanguageSpecificPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Tokio broadcast channel calls clone in parallel, but does not require `Sync`"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The broadcast channel internally calls `clone` on the stored value when receiving it, and only requires `T:Send`. This means that using the broadcast channel with values that are `Send` but not `Sync` can trigger unsoundness if the `clone` implementation makes use of the value being `!Sync`.\n\nThank you to Austin Bonander for finding and reporting this issue."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-rr8g-9fpq-6wmg",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability GHSA-rr8g-9fpq-6wmg\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: tokio\nFixed Version: 1.44.2, 1.38.2, 1.43.1\nLink: [GHSA-rr8g-9fpq-6wmg](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-rr8g-9fpq-6wmg)\nThe broadcast channel internally calls `clone` on the stored value when receiving it, and only requires `T:Send`. This means that using the broadcast channel with values that are `Send` but not `Sync` can trigger unsoundness if the `clone` implementation makes use of the value being `!Sync`.\n\nThank you to Austin Bonander for finding and reporting this issue.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability GHSA-rr8g-9fpq-6wmg**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|tokio|1.44.2, 1.38.2, 1.43.1|[GHSA-rr8g-9fpq-6wmg](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-rr8g-9fpq-6wmg)|\n\nThe broadcast channel internally calls `clone` on the stored value when receiving it, and only requires `T:Send`. This means that using the broadcast channel with values that are `Send` but not `Sync` can trigger unsoundness if the `clone` implementation makes use of the value being `!Sync`.\n\nThank you to Austin Bonander for finding and reporting this issue."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cvssv40_baseScore": 2.7,
                "cvssv40_vector": "CVSS:4.0/AV:N/AC:L/AT:N/PR:N/UI:N/VC:N/VI:L/VA:N/SC:N/SI:N/SA:N/E:U",
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "2.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "GHSA-8qv2-5vq6-g2g7",
              "name": "LanguageSpecificPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "webpki: CPU denial of service in certificate path building"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "When this crate is given a pathological certificate chain to validate, it will spend CPU time exponential with the number of candidate certificates at each step of path building.\n\nBoth TLS clients and TLS servers that accept client certificate are affected.\n\nThis was previously reported in https://github.com/briansmith/webpki/issues/69.\n\n`rustls-webpki` is a fork of this crate which contains a fix for this issue and is actively maintained."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-8qv2-5vq6-g2g7",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability GHSA-8qv2-5vq6-g2g7\nSeverity: HIGH\nPackage: webpki\nFixed Version: 0.22.2\nLink: [GHSA-8qv2-5vq6-g2g7](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-8qv2-5vq6-g2g7)\nWhen this crate is given a pathological certificate chain to validate, it will spend CPU time exponential with the number of candidate certificates at each step of path building.\n\nBoth TLS clients and TLS servers that accept client certificate are affected.\n\nThis was previously reported in https://github.com/briansmith/webpki/issues/69.\n\n`rustls-webpki` is a fork of this crate which contains a fix for this issue and is actively maintained.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability GHSA-8qv2-5vq6-g2g7**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|HIGH|webpki|0.22.2|[GHSA-8qv2-5vq6-g2g7](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-8qv2-5vq6-g2g7)|\n\nWhen this crate is given a pathological certificate chain to validate, it will spend CPU time exponential with the number of candidate certificates at each step of path building.\n\nBoth TLS clients and TLS servers that accept client certificate are affected.\n\nThis was previously reported in https://github.com/briansmith/webpki/issues/69.\n\n`rustls-webpki` is a fork of this crate which contains a fix for this issue and is actively maintained."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cvssv3_baseScore": 7.5,
                "cvssv3_vector": "CVSS:3.0/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:H",
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "HIGH"
                ]
              }
            }
          ],
          "version": "0.71.2"
        }
      },
